{# ============================================================================
   THE WORDS THIS AREA'S GATING QUESTIONS OFFER — the design's lists.html, ported.

   IT IS A SECTION OF THE CONFIGURE PAGE that kept an address of its own, beside
   Incident kinds, so it wears the configure page's heading and the configure
   page's strip and the Configure action stays lit on it. The shell draws all
   three; nothing here draws navigation, and there is no way back other than the
   strip, the lit Configure and the crumb.

   FOUR FOLDS, ONE OPEN, AND THE FOLD IS A SPACE SAVER. Four full lists on one
   page would not fit, so three are shut — and a shut fold here never means "you
   may leave this alone", which is why its summary counts the sub-categories that
   READ the list and carries OPEN / CLOSED in a word beside the caret. That is
   deliberately not the report form's fold wording, where a shut block genuinely
   may be optional.

   THE OPEN ONE IS WHICHEVER LIST THE ROUTE RESOLVES, so every control on the page
   is a link or a small server-rendered form carrying the CSRF token and the whole
   screen works with no JavaScript — including the row being renamed, which
   survives the round trip because the fold it is in is in the URL.

   `panels`, `editing`, `recordScreens`, `csrfToken`, `area` come from
   IncidentAreaListController.
   ========================================================================== #}
{% extends '@UhifadhiIncident/base.html.twig' %}

{% block title %}Incidents · lists{% endblock %}

{% block stylesheets %}
    {{ parent() }}
    {# The configure sections' own `tx-` vocabulary and this screen's registry
       rows, by the bundle's own constant so the path is never typed twice. #}
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ asset(constant('Uhifadhi\\Incident\\UhifadhiIncidentBundle::TAXONOMY_STYLESHEET')) }}">
{% endblock %}

{% block incident_trail_tail %} <a href="{{ path('incident_dashboard', {uuid: area.uuid}) }}">incidents</a> / lists{% endblock %}

{% block shell_page_title %}{{ area.name }} &mdash; Incidents &middot; configure{% endblock %}

{% block shell_page_subtitle %}Everything this module is set up with in <b>this area</b>, in one place: how its dashboard is composed, the kinds of incident this area files, the words its questions offer, and how money and terms behave under them.{% endblock %}

{% block shell_page_actions %}
    {% if recordScreens %}
        <a class="cta" href="{{ path('incident_new', {uuid: area.uuid}) }}">{{ ux_icon('incident:pencil') }} Report incident</a>
    {% endif %}
{% endblock %}

{% block incident_page %}
<div class="tx-lists">
    {# Scope and authority, said once and plainly — the two facts that change how
       the whole page is read: the words are this area's, and naming them is not
       something filing an incident earns. #}
    <div class="tx-say">{{ ux_icon('incident:shield', {width: 17, height: 17}) }}<div><b>These lists are {{ area.name }}&rsquo;s own.</b>
        A list is not a kind of incident &mdash; it is the <b>words one question offers</b>, and they are this area&rsquo;s words.
        Two records about the same water body are countable as one place, and two about the same animal as one species, which a
        typed name can never promise. Editing rides on <span class="mono">incidents.manage</span> &mdash; the same authority that
        names the kinds. Filing an incident is not enough to name the words everybody else must pick from.</div></div>

    <div class="use"><b>There are exactly four lists, and an area cannot add a fifth.</b> Each one belongs to a question a
        behaviour block already asks &mdash; the species, the method, the land use and the named place &mdash; so a new list
        arrives only with a new block, in code. What is this area&rsquo;s is <b>the words in them</b>, and every other question on
        the report form either carries its own fixed choices or is typed. <b>The four folds below are a space saver and nothing
        more:</b> every one of these lists is in use, and a shut fold says only that four full lists on one page would not fit.</div>

    {% for panel in panels %}
        {{ include('@UhifadhiIncident/lists/_fold.html.twig', {
            area: area,
            panel: panel,
            editing: editing,
            csrfToken: csrfToken,
        }, with_context: false) }}
    {% endfor %}

    {# WHAT RETIRING DOES, said where somebody is about to press Retire. The
       design prints a workshop index in this card's tab; that is the design
       workspace's referencing system and it does not ship. #}
    <div class="c">
        <span class="tab">What retiring an entry does<span class="src">&middot; never delete</span></span>
        <div class="use"><b>Retired leaves the picker and stays on the record.</b> Exactly as an observation kind or a
            sub-category does: every incident already filed under the word keeps it, it stays in the register filter, in every
            export and on every case file, and it is off the report form and off the handsets at their next sync. Bring it back
            in one click and it returns as it was. <b>There is no delete control on this page</b>, so nothing here can orphan a
            case file. A rename is safe for the same reason the kinds are: the entry keeps the identity the filed records point
            at, and only its words change.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="use whose"><b>Whose lists these are.</b> They are held per area and edited here, in the incidents module,
        because every one of them is read by a question the module asks. The model is written so the same words could later be
        lifted to the area&rsquo;s own shared vocabulary &mdash; a species list a second module could read &mdash; without moving
        them again; until a second module needs them, the screen stays the module&rsquo;s.</div>
</div>
{% endblock %}
